<title>USA: USDA to change animal product export rules.</title>
<headline>USDA to change animal product export rules.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-09-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>The U.S. Department of Agriculture said Wednesday it proposed to allow the issuance of export certificates for animal products without inspection, when inspection is not required by the destination country.</p>
<p>The change would make it easier for U.S. exporters to sell products outside the United States that do not require inspection, USDA said.</p>
<p>Most countries require that imported animal products be accompanied by an export certificate, but often there is no requirement for inspection, USDA said. Currently, USDA regulations do not allow issuance of export certificates for uninspected animal products.</p>
<p>In a proposed regulation to be published in the Federal Register on September 19, the department would also amend its animal byproduct regulations to replace references to &quot;inedible animal products&quot; with &quot;animal products,&quot; to further facilitate exports, USDA said.</p>
<p>-- Washington Commodities Desk (202) 898-8394</p>
